---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Limitations"
  description="The explicit limits of browser compatibility evidence and ControlCurrent calculations."
>
  <header class="shell page-header">
    <span class="eyebrow">Use the result as one input</span>
    <h1>Compatibility is not a security verdict</h1>
    <p>
      ControlCurrent evaluates documented feature availability. It cannot inspect, test, approve, or
      certify an application deployment.
    </p>
  </header>
  <article class="shell prose">
    <h2>Source limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        BCD is maintained compatibility data and can contain corrections or incomplete statements.
      </li>
      <li>
        A support statement does not prove complete specification conformance or absence of defects.
      </li>
      <li>
        The catalogue covers nine desktop and mobile browser families, not every browser, operating
        system version, vendor shell, or embedded runtime.
      </li>
      <li>
        Mobile-family data may be mirrored from a related engine upstream and does not prove
        identical behaviour on every device.
      </li>
      <li>WebDX Baseline is adoptability context, not deployment or configuration assurance.</li>
      <li>
        WPT suite mappings identify relevant conformance tests, but the registry does not ingest or
        simplify live pass rates.
      </li>
      <li>
        A passing conformance test does not establish correct application configuration, complete
        control coverage, or absence of browser defects.
      </li>
      <li>Lower-level BCD paths can change and require deliberate catalogue review.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Calculation limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Version comparison covers the numeric browser release forms used by the selected paths.
      </li>
      <li>Imprecise and unparseable support versions remain unknown or qualified.</li>
      <li>
        A multi-path mapping reflects the project-authored definition of a deployable control.
      </li>
      <li>Unsupported mappings are shown rather than approximated from a related feature.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Security limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No website is scanned and no runtime behaviour is observed.</li>
      <li>
        The optional assessment sees only supplied evidence. A bundle can reveal variation among
        included routes, applies explicit per-surface requirements, and can identify missing
        evidence from a declared surface manifest. It cannot discover omitted routes, redirects,
        status codes, cached responses, user states, or environments.
      </li>
      <li>
        CSP can be correlated with supplied inline markup and bounded local resource bytes can be
        checked against SRI metadata. Nothing is fetched, and no result establishes remote resource
        identity, complete correctness, rollout safety, or effective browser enforcement.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fetch Metadata and WebAuthn inputs describe reduced configuration evidence; the tool does
        not test server enforcement, authenticator behaviour, or ceremony outcomes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Application, environment, revision, build, producer, and capture-time fields are
        fingerprinted assertions. An optional CLI gate can authenticate a matching signed statement
        from one exact Sigstore certificate identity, but it cannot prove that the producer's
        collection was complete or truthful.
      </li>
      <li>Fallback controls remain necessary for unsupported browsers and defence in depth.</li>
      <li>No result should be treated as legal, compliance, or certification advice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Practical usefulness</h2>
    <p>These are project self-assessments, not benchmark scores.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>9.7/10 for focused education and browser-policy planning:</strong> exact BCD, Baseline,
        WPT, compatibility-policy, deployment-evidence, and evidence-policy layers are kept distinct.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>9.8/10 as an engineering decision aid:</strong> explicit surface applicability, subject-identified
        fingerprints, application and environment comparison boundaries, revision-aware freshness policy,
        exact Sigstore signer policy, detail-aware comparisons, bounded exceptions, local export, and
        transparent composites support design and release decisions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>6.8/10 as production security assurance:</strong> supplied policy, markup, request, WebAuthn,
        and local resource evidence can reveal real configuration gaps; the independent gate now rejects
        the wrong application, environment, revision, producer class, capture duration, evidence age,
        report digest, and verified signer identity. There is still no authenticated collection, discovered
        route inventory, runtime enforcement test, remote resource verification, adversarial test, or
        continuous monitoring.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
